  { event, level, ts, sessionId, userHash?, release, ...fields }

  [38;5;153mevent[39m is a stable dot-delimited string ([38;5;153mlogin.submit[39m, [38;5;153mlogin.validation_failed[39m, [38;5;153mlogin.error[39m, [38;5;153mapp.unhandled_error[39m), not
  a prose sentence — prose is what makes logs unsearchable a month later. [38;5;153msessionId[39m is a random id minted per page load,
  which is what actually lets you reconstruct one user's sequence of events.

  The redaction rule is a two-part table in [38;5;153mredact.js[39m:

  - [1mField allowlist[0m — a [38;5;153mSet[39m of permitted key names ([38;5;153mevent[39m, [38;5;153mlevel[39m, [38;5;153mts[39m, [38;5;153msessionId[39m, [38;5;153muserHash[39m, [38;5;153mrelease[39m, [38;5;153merrorName[39m,
    [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m, [38;5;153mdurationMs[39m, [38;5;153mhttpStatus[39m, [38;5;153mformField[39m). Any key not in the set is dropped, and the logger records a
    [38;5;153mredact.dropped_field[39m counter with the [3mkey name only[0m so you can tell when a call site is trying to send something it
    shouldn't.
  - [1mHard denylist on values[0m — keys matching [38;5;153m/pass|pwd|secret|token|auth|cookie/i[39m are dropped before the allowlist even
    runs, so a future rename can't accidentally allowlist a credential.

  [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m is the one field I want to flag as a judgement call. Error messages are enormously useful and are also
  the single most common place user data leaks into logs (a validation error that quotes the input, a server message
  echoing an email). I'd include it but truncate to 200 characters and run it through a scrubber for anything that looks
  like an email address or a long digit run. That's mitigation, not a guarantee — if you'd rather, we drop [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m
  entirely and keep only [38;5;153merrorName[39m, which is leak-proof and substantially less useful.

  On the user hash, written accurately this time: [38;5;153muserHash = sha256(salt + username)[39m truncated to 16 hex chars, where
  [38;5;153msalt[39m is a per-deployment constant compiled into the bundle. It gives you stable correlation across a session and
  across reports. It does [1mnot[0m make the value unlinkable — the salt ships in client code, and usernames are guessable —
  so the spec will describe it as a pseudonymous correlation id, and your privacy documentation should treat the log
  store as containing pseudonymized personal data rather than anonymous data.

  Two open decisions in this section, both cheap to reverse later:

  1. [38;5;153merrorMessage[39m truncated-and-scrubbed, or [38;5;153merrorName[39m only?
  2. Does the hashed-username limitation change your mind toward the per-browser random id instead?

  Does the record shape look right, and how do you want those two?

